<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide NuGet vulnerability audit.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    SSH.NET 2024.2.0 shipped GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284 (HIGH) in the SFTP delivery path and rode
    every single restore for months as `warning NU1903`. Nothing consumed that warning: CI's
    `dotnet restore ProcuLink.slnx` step is green with it, the Docker build is green with it,
    and no human reads restore output. A warning nobody sees is not a control. Escalating the
    HIGH/CRITICAL audit codes to errors is what turns it into one.

    NU1901 low · NU1902 moderate · NU1903 high · NU1904 critical.
    NuGetAuditLevel=high already suppresses NU1901/NU1902, so only 1903/1904 can ever fire.

    WHY MODE IS `all`
    This started at `direct` because two HIGH advisories lived in TRANSITIVE packages that no
    first-party change could clear at the time, and under `all` they fail the build on a clean
    checkout — including the Railway Docker image build, which would mean no deploys. Both are
    now cleared, so the audit covers the whole graph:

      · Newtonsoft.Json 11.0.1 (GHSA-5crp-9r3c-p9vr) arrived via Hangfire.Core, whose
        netstandard2.0 dependency group declares `Newtonsoft.Json >= 11.0.1`. That floor is
        unchanged in the newest Hangfire.Core (1.8.24 verified), so no parent upgrade clears
        it — ProcuLink.Infrastructure carries a direct pin to 13.0.3 instead, explained there.
      · System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 8.0.2 (eight HIGH advisories) arrived via
        Microsoft.AspNetCore.DataProtection.EntityFrameworkCore 8.0.11. Upgrading that parent
        to 8.0.30 binds 8.0.4, which clears all eight; the EF Core pins moved to 8.0.30 in
        lockstep because 8.0.30 requires it.

    `direct` would have covered only the class the SSH.NET finding belonged to: a direct
    <PackageReference> with a known HIGH advisory, upgradable by definition. Neither of the two
    above is in that class, and both were real. Re-check the whole graph with
    `dotnet list ProcuLink.slnx package` plus the `vulnerable` and `include-transitive`
    switches — that is the command that tells you whether a fix is available before you weaken
    anything here.

    `all` DOES NOTHING ON THE .NET 8 SDK — READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THIS FILE.
    NuGet 6.11, which .NET SDK 8.0.4xx ships, audits DIRECT PackageReferences only and reads
    neither value of this property; transitive coverage arrives with NuGet 6.12 / the .NET 9
    SDK. Both Dockerfiles build on `mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0` (8.0.424 / NuGet 6.11.2
    at the time of writing), so THE RAILWAY IMAGE BUILD CANNOT FAIL ON A TRANSITIVE ADVISORY
    no matter what this file says.

    Measured, not assumed — mutation on the real deploy path, 2026-08-15:
      · Revert DataProtection.EntityFrameworkCore to 8.0.11 (drags in the vulnerable
        transitive System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 8.0.2): `docker build -f Dockerfile .`
        is GREEN, zero NU19xx lines, with the restore layer verified re-run from a cold
        cache. The same tree is RED locally under the .NET 10 SDK, 40 NU1903 errors.
      · Revert SSH.NET to 2024.2.0 (a vulnerable DIRECT reference): the same Docker build
        FAILS with `error NU1903`. Direct auditing works there; only the mode is ignored.

    CI's SDK is NOT the 8.0.424 that its `setup-dotnet` step installs, and is not pinned by
    anything in this repo: global.json asks for 8.0.100 with `rollForward: latestMajor`, so
    `dotnet` selects the highest SDK present on the runner image. The evidence that this
    already happens is that CI restores ProcuLink.slnx successfully while 8.0.424 cannot —
    `dotnet restore ProcuLink.slnx` inside the sdk:8.0 container fails with
    `error MSB4068: The element <Solution> is unrecognized`, because .slnx support post-dates
    that SDK. So CI probably DOES enforce `all` today, on an SDK chosen by GitHub's image
    contents rather than by us, and that can change under the repo without a commit.

    A control that depends on which SDK a runner image happened to ship is not a control.
    That is why CI also carries an explicit `Audit transitive NuGet advisories` step running
    `dotnet list ProcuLink.slnx package` with the `vulnerable` and `include-transitive`
    switches and failing on High/Critical. It holds the transitive half on every SDK.
    (Spelling those switches with their leading dashes is what breaks this file: an XML
    comment may not contain a double hyphen, and MSBuild answers with a parse error on
    Directory.Build.props that reads nothing like the cause.)

    Keeping the property at `all` is still right: it is the correct declaration, it protects
    developer machines today, and it enforces itself wherever the SDK is new enough. Just do
    not read a green Docker build as proof the graph is clean.

    ESCAPE HATCH: if a HIGH advisory lands with no fixed version yet and a deploy cannot wait,
    build with `-p:NuGetAudit=false` and open an issue the same day. Do not delete this file
    and do not quietly drop the mode back to `direct` to unblock yourself — `direct` hides
    exactly the two findings above, which took a parent upgrade and a transitive pin to fix.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>high</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
